            %p
              For notational brevity we will be referring to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi W
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{W}G
              in the following, but it may be helpful to keep thinking of the nerve of the delooping groupoid. From that perspective, an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-simplex"} n-simplex
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi W
                        %mo ¯
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{W}G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-tuple"} n-tuple
              of group elements, is suggestively denoted as a sequence of composable arrows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi W
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mi n
